## DATA TRAPS (we hit all of these before; check for each one)
1. The file's "Total Volume" is inflated. Google repeats one volume across near-identical phrases, so summing them counts the same searches many times (one file's total was about 1.37M when the real figure was about 718K). Use each cluster's PEAK (highest single keyword volume) instead, counted once.
2. A merged head term can inflate a cluster. Example: "fingernail fungus" showed 90,500 but that was Google merging it with the generic "nail fungus"; the specific phrase was 9,900. When a cluster's peak is 5x or more its own name's volume, look inside it and correct it by hand, and tell me you did.
3. Wrong-topic and noise keywords sneak in. Exclude and list them: other conditions (ingrown hair, dog nails), medical billing codes (ICD, CPT), other languages, country-specific pricing (NHS, Kaiser, Pakistan), brand-only queries, meme or video-only queries, and generic single words. Show me the excluded list so I can veto it.
4. Many clusters show 0 volume because the tool returned no estimate, not because nobody searches. Read 0 as "small, unmeasured", never as "none".
5. CPC is mostly 0 in these files. Never call a topic "high CPC" unless the CPC column supports it. Report real CPC where it exists (for example "is nail fungus contagious" was $2.42) and say "no CPC data" where it does not.
6. Volumes are Google-style estimates and are not data from our own readers. Say "people search for", never "our readers ask".
7. Assign every cluster to exactly ONE post or topic so no search is counted twice.

